<%# The guide. Outside the chrome deliberately: changing channel replaces the chrome, and
    a listing that vanished every time you tuned from it would be no use as a listing. It
    stays up, the picture in its corner changes, and the highlight moves.

    Empty until it is opened. The grid is fetched then, because it is only ever wanted on
    purpose and because a grid built into the page would go stale while a film played. %>
<div class="tvguide" data-cable-guide-target="panel" hidden>
  <div class="tvguide__head">
    <%# The player is not moved into here -- moving an iframe in the DOM reloads it, and
        the whole point is that what you were watching keeps playing. The frame stack is
        resized into this corner by the stylesheet instead; this is the hole it sits in. %>
    <div class="tvguide__preview"></div>

    <%# What the panel describes follows the pointer around the grid, and falls back to
        what is actually playing when the pointer stops or leaves. Both links lead out of
        cable: the channel to its own page, the programme to the ordinary watch player,
        where it plays from the beginning and counts towards what you have seen. Neither
        carries data-cinema-move, so they are ordinary navigations rather than a tune. %>
    <div class="tvguide__detail">
      <%# The channel, by number and name, the way it is said everywhere else on the box. %>
      <a class="tvguide__detail-channel" data-cable-guide-target="detailChannel" href="#"></a>
      <h2 class="tvguide__detail-title">
        <a data-cable-guide-target="detailTitle" href="#"></a>
      </h2>
      <%# Which episode, and what it is called. The grid says only the show -- an
          afternoon of one series would otherwise be a column of identical cells -- so
          this is the only place the rest of it is said. Empty, and gone, for a film. %>
      <p class="tvguide__detail-episode" data-cable-guide-target="detailEpisode" hidden></p>

      <%# One line of facts, each its own element so the two marks can sit against the
          scores they belong to. The dots between them are drawn by the stylesheet from
          whichever parts are showing, so a film with no rating does not leave one
          stranded. %>
      <p class="tvguide__detail-meta">
        <span data-cable-guide-target="detailWhen" hidden></span>
        <span data-cable-guide-target="detailRuntime" hidden></span>
        <span data-cable-guide-target="detailYear" hidden></span>
        <span class="tvguide__score tvguide__score--imdb" data-cable-guide-target="detailImdb" hidden>
          <i class="fa-brands fa-imdb"></i>
          <span data-cable-guide-target="detailImdbScore"></span>
        </span>
        <%# Out of cable, like the other two links in this panel: it opens Letterboxd's
            own review prompt in a new tab. The score beside it is this member's own, and
            is absent until they have logged one. %>
        <a class="tvguide__score tvguide__score--letterboxd" data-cable-guide-target="detailLetterboxd"
           href="#" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" hidden>
          <i class="fa-brands fa-square-letterboxd"></i>
          <span data-cable-guide-target="detailLetterboxdScore"></span>
        </a>
      </p>

      <p class="tvguide__detail-genre" data-cable-guide-target="detailGenre" hidden></p>

      <%# The same address the title above carries, said as a button. Pointing at a cell
          halfway through an afternoon and wanting the film from the top is most of what
          anybody opens the guide to do, and a heading that happens to be a link is not a
          way to offer it. Out of cable: the ordinary watch player, from the beginning,
          counting towards what you have seen. No data-cinema-move, so it navigates
          rather than tuning. %>
      <p class="tvguide__detail-actions">
        <a class="tvguide__start" data-cable-guide-target="detailStart" href="#">
          <i class="fa-solid fa-backward-fast"></i>
          <span>Start from the beginning</span>
        </a>
      </p>

      <%# Two marks that are also two switches. Both are drawn hollow for off and solid
          for on, which is the whole of what they say -- there is no room beside a
          picture for a word as well, and the pair of glyphs is the same pair the rest of
          the site uses for these two states.

          Absent for a guest: neither has anywhere to record an answer. %>
      <% if user_signed_in? %>
        <p class="tvguide__detail-marks">
          <%# Marking something watched is the one thing on /cable that writes, and it
              goes through the ordinary entries#complete route -- the same one the banner
              below uses. A thing the viewer chose to do, not a side effect of the page
              having been rendered. %>
          <button type="button" class="tvguide__mark" data-cable-guide-target="watched"
                  data-action="click->cable-guide#watch" title="Mark as watched">
            <i class="fa-regular fa-eye" data-cable-guide-target="watchedIcon"></i>
          </button>

          <%# Files a copy of the film in this member's own favourites channel, and takes
              it out again. A copy, because an entry belongs to one channel -- so
              favouriting cannot move a film off the channel that was playing it, and
              un-favouriting removes the copy rather than the original. %>
          <button type="button" class="tvguide__mark" data-cable-guide-target="favorite"
                  data-action="click->cable-guide#favorite" title="Add to my favourites">
            <i class="fa-regular fa-heart" data-cable-guide-target="favoriteIcon"></i>
          </button>
        </p>
      <% end %>

      <p class="tvguide__detail-plot" data-cable-guide-target="detailPlot"></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# The corner the box kept its own buttons in. %>
  <div class="tvguide__controls">
    <%# The dial's settings, on /admin/cable. Admin only, and on the same terms as /admin:
        the check is on the real account, and it is gone while viewing the site as
        somebody else. Left of Home because it is the rarer of the two and Home is the one
        the eye goes to the corner for.

        A link rather than the rebuild button this used to be. One button over a playing
        picture could only ever be the one thing, which made dealing the schedule again
        the only thing an admin could do to the dial -- the line-up and its order were a
        console job. Rebuilding is on that page now, next to them. %>
    <% if true_user&.admin? && !impersonating? %>
      <%= link_to admin_cable_path, class: "tvguide__settings",
                  title: "The dial: which channels are on it, in what order, and the schedule" do %>
        <i class="fa-solid fa-sliders"></i>
        <span>Settings</span>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>

    <%# The way out of cable altogether. The banner has one of these, but the guide covers
        the whole screen while it is up and the banner with it, so the guide needs its own
        or the only way off the channel is the browser's back button. %>
    <%= link_to root_path, class: "tvguide__home", title: "Home" do %>
      <i class="fa-solid fa-house"></i>
      <span>Home</span>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <button type="button" class="tvguide__hide" data-action="click->cable-guide#close">
    <i class="fa-solid fa-compress-alt"></i> Hide Guide
  </button>

  <%# The scrolling element, so the scroll listener has to be here -- scroll does not
      bubble, and the guide needs to know the viewer is awake to stop the grid hauling
      itself back to the present under somebody reading tomorrow evening. %>
  <div class="tvguide__body" data-cable-guide-target="body"
       data-action="scroll->cable-guide#scrolled">
    <p class="tvguide__loading">Loading listings&hellip;</p>
  </div>
</div>
